INT. ORBITAL SEED VAULT - HOLD C - CONTINUOUS

A cold, white corridor of sealed drawers, each labeled in a script that has gone out of use. Frost on the rails. A low hum of cooling pumps.

KAZIMIR, 50s, in a pressure suit with the helmet off, pushes a cart slowly down the aisle. His breath clouds.

TOLLIVER, 40s, also in a suit, strides ahead with a tablet. He stops at a drawer. Frowns.

TOLLIVER
This one is warm.

KAZIMIR
Warm?

TOLLIVER
Eleven degrees above spec.

KAZIMIR
It's the cooling pump. I told them in March.

TOLLIVER
He told us in March. We told Command.

KAZIMIR
Command said what?

TOLLIVER
That it was within tolerance.

Kazimir sighs. Parks the cart. Opens the drawer. Frost curls out.

Inside, hundreds of tiny glass vials, row on row. Some are dark. Some are faintly green.

KAZIMIR
How many have turned?

TOLLIVER
Sixty. Maybe seventy.

KAZIMIR
Out of how many?

TOLLIVER
Eleven thousand.

Kazimir lifts one vial. Holds it to the light. A single green seed curls inside, soft and alive.

KAZIMIR
Still breathing.

TOLLIVER
Is it?

KAZIMIR
Look at it. It's trying.

Tolliver sets down the tablet. Takes the vial. Cups it in both gloved hands. For a long time he says nothing.

TOLLIVER
When I was young, he told me nothing in a vault ever grows.

KAZIMIR
He was wrong.

TOLLIVER
He was often wrong.

Kazimir takes the vial back. Places it in a tray labeled RESCUE. Closes the drawer.

KAZIMIR
We start with this one. We fix the pump. We save sixty.

TOLLIVER
And the rest?

KAZIMIR
We tell them it was within tolerance.

Both laugh, softly. The cooling pumps hum.
